<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Emerg Infect Dis</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">eid</journal-id><journal-title>Emerging Infectious Diseases</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1080-6040</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1080-6059</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10081682</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2627684</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Proficiency of clinical laboratories in and near Monterrey, Mexico, to detect vancomycin-resistant enterococci.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>McDonald</surname><given-names>L. C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Garza</surname><given-names>L. R.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Jarvis</surname><given-names>W. R.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Atlanta, Georgia 30333, USA.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jan-Feb</season><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>5</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>143</fpage><lpage>146</lpage><abstract><p>Early detection of vancomycin-resistant enterococci is important for preventing its spread among hospitalized patients. We surveyed the ability of eight hospital laboratories in and near Monterrey, Mexico, to detect vancomycin resistance in Enterococcus spp. and found that although laboratories can reliably detect high-level vancomycin resistance, many have difficulty detecting low-level resistance.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>